---

<BaseLayout
  title="Design tokens"
  description="The Atelier UI design token system — colors, spacing, radii, and typography primitives, authored in code and projected into Figma and every component."
  currentPath="/tokens"
  tocItems={[
    { id: 'tiers', label: 'How the tiers fit together' },
    { id: 'colors', label: 'Colors' },
    { id: 'spacing', label: 'Spacing' },
    { id: 'border-radius', label: 'Border radius' },
    { id: 'typography-scale', label: 'Typography scale' },
    { id: 'shadows', label: 'Shadows' },
    { id: 'motion', label: 'Motion' },
    { id: 'pipeline', label: 'The token pipeline in practice' },
    { id: 'pink-test', label: 'Exercise: the pink test' },
  ]}
>
  <div class="docs-inline-page">
    <PageHero title="Design tokens" titleAccent="tokens" ledeMax="520px">
      <strong>Tokens, not utilities.</strong> All CSS custom properties used by
      Atelier UI — colors, spacing, radius, typography, shadows, and motion.
      Import once via <code>tokens.css</code>; dark and light mode update
      automatically.
    </PageHero>

    <Callout tone="tip" title="Watch tokens swap live.">
      Use the theme toggle in the topbar to flip between light and dark — every
      swatch and value below updates instantly. In your own app, set
      <code>data-theme="dark"</code> on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> to force dark
      mode, <code>data-theme="light"</code> to force light. With neither
      attribute set, the user’s OS preference wins.
    </Callout>

    <!-- Architecture -->
    <div class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="tiers" class="docs-section-title">How the tiers fit together</h2>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        Tokens are layered, and each layer only ever points one step down. Raw
        <strong>values</strong> live in <strong>Primitives</strong> (a palette
        like <code>teal/500</code>). <strong>Meaning</strong> lives in the
        <strong>UI tier</strong> — the <code>--ui-*</code> tokens on this page —
        which alias a primitive and decide what it is <em>for</em>
        (<code>color/primary</code>). This is also the only tier that carries
        light and dark modes. On top sit <strong>Component tokens</strong>,
        component-local names (<code>button/bg-primary</code>) that alias a UI
        token.
      </p>

      <div
        style="
          display: flex;
          align-items: stretch;
          gap: 0;
          flex-wrap: wrap;
          border: 1px solid var(--ui-color-border);
          border-radius: var(--ui-radius-lg);
          overflow: hidden;
          background: var(--ui-color-surface-raised);
        "
      >
        {[
          {
            tier: 'Primitive',
            sub: 'raw value · single mode',
            ex: 'teal/500',
            val: '#006470',
          },
          {
            tier: 'UI (semantic)',
            sub: 'meaning · light + dark',
            ex: 'color/primary',
            val: '--ui-color-primary',
          },
          {
            tier: 'Component',
            sub: 'component role · Figma only',
            ex: 'button/bg-primary',
            val: 'aliases color/primary',
          },
        ].map((step, i) => <div
          style="
            flex: 1 1 180px;
            padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
            border-left: 1px solid var(--ui-color-border);
            position: relative;
          "
        >
          <div
            style="
              font-size: var(--ui-font-size-xs);
              font-weight: 700;
              text-transform: uppercase;
              letter-spacing: 0.06em;
              color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
            "
          >
            {`Tier ${i + 1}`}
          </div>
          <div
            style="
              font-size: 0.92rem;
              font-weight: 600;
              color: var(--ui-color-text);
              margin: 0.15rem 0 0.1rem;
            "
          >
            {step.tier}
          </div>
          <div
            style="
              font-size: var(--ui-font-size-sm);
              color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
              margin-bottom: 0.7rem;
            "
          >
            {step.sub}
          </div>
          <div
            style="
              font-family: monospace;
              font-size: var(--ui-font-size-xs);
              font-weight: 600;
              color: var(--ui-color-primary);
            "
          >
            {step.ex}
          </div>
          <div
            style="
              font-family: monospace;
              font-size: var(--ui-font-size-xs);
              color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
              margin-top: 0.15rem;
            "
          >
            {step.val}
          </div>
          {i < 2 && <span
            aria-hidden="true"
            style="
              position: absolute;
              top: 1.1rem;
              right: -0.55rem;
              font-size: 0.9rem;
              color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
              background: var(--ui-color-surface-raised);
              padding: 0 0.1rem;
              z-index: 1;
            "
            >→</span
          >}
        </div>)}
      </div>

      <Callout tone="note" title="Figma has three tiers, the CSS has two.">
        The code (<code>tokens.css</code>) materializes only the UI tier as
        <code>--ui-*</code>, and components reference those directly — there is
        no <code>--button-bg-primary</code> CSS layer. The Component tier exists
        in the Figma file as a handoff and teaching aid, but it is
        <strong>not</strong> projected into code. So if you map a Figma
        <code>button/bg-primary</code> looking for its CSS twin, you will only
        find the UI token it aliases. That asymmetry is deliberate — the
        reasoning is recorded in <code>ADR-0018</code>
        (<code>plan/adr/0018-figma-token-tiering-and-css-projection.md</code>).
      </Callout>
    </div>

    <!-- Transferable: naming rules -->
    <div class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="naming" class="docs-section-title">
        Naming tokens for your own system
      </h2>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        The value catalogue below is Atelier's. What carries over to your system
        is the rules behind the names, because an agent picks a token by reading
        its name. A name that states a <em>role</em> lets it choose correctly; a
        name that states a <em>value</em> forces it to guess.
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3">Name the role, not the value</h3>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        Atelier keeps both kinds, in different tiers. The primitive ramp is
        named for the value (<code>--ui-color-teal-700</code>). The tokens a
        component reads are named for the job: <code>--ui-color-primary</code>
        aliases <code>teal-700</code> in light mode and <code>teal-300</code> in
        dark mode. Asked for "the muted text colour", an agent finds
        <code>--ui-color-text-muted</code> by name and gets the right result in
        both modes. Asked the same with only <code>--ui-gray-500</code>
        available, it has to guess which grey is the secondary text, and the
        grey that works on a white page fails on a dark one.
      </p>
      <table class="docs-props-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Value name (avoid in components)</th>
            <th>Role name (what components read)</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>--ui-color-teal-700</code></td>
            <td><code>--ui-color-primary</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>--ui-color-red-800</code></td>
            <td><code>--ui-color-danger-text</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>--ui-color-red-100</code></td>
            <td><code>--ui-color-danger-bg</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>#475569</code></td>
            <td><code>--ui-color-text-muted</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin: 1rem 0;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        Pattern:
        <code>--ui-color-&lt;role&gt;[-&lt;part&gt;][-&lt;state&gt;]</code>, for
        example <code>text-on-primary</code> (the text that sits on the primary
        fill) or <code>input-border-invalid</code>. Keep the ramp for the one
        place that must say "this exact teal".
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">State suffixes</h3>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        A state is a suffix on the role it modifies, never a new colour name.
        These are the suffixes <code>tokens.css</code> uses today:
      </p>
      <table class="docs-props-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>State</th>
            <th>Real tokens</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>hover</td>
            <td>
              <code>--ui-color-primary-hover</code>,
              <code>--ui-color-danger-hover</code>,
              <code>--ui-color-border-hover</code>,
              <code>--ui-color-input-border-hover</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>active (pressed)</td>
            <td>
              <code>--ui-color-primary-active</code>,
              <code>--ui-color-danger-active</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>focus</td>
            <td>
              <code>--ui-color-input-bg-focus</code>,
              <code>--ui-color-input-border-focus</code>, and the ring itself,
              <code>--ui-focus-ring</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>invalid</td>
            <td>
              <code>--ui-color-input-border-invalid</code>,
              <code>--ui-focus-ring-danger</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>disabled</td>
            <td>
              <code>--ui-color-disabled-bg</code>,
              <code>--ui-color-disabled-text</code>,
              <code>--ui-color-disabled-border</code>
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin: 1rem 0;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        Check your own system for the gap this table makes visible: a state a
        component needs but no token names. That is how the knockout example
        below started.
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">Light and dark mapping</h3>
      <ul
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.7;
          margin: 0 0 1rem;
          padding-left: 1.25rem;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)"
            >Same names in both modes.</strong
          >
          Only the values differ. Atelier declares light values on
          <code>:root</code> and overrides the same names under
          <code>@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)</code> and
          <code>[data-theme="dark"]</code>; no component branches on the mode.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)"
            >Map by role, not by flipping the ramp.</strong
          >
          Primary is <code>teal-700</code> in light and <code>teal-300</code> in
          dark, and its hover moves the other way round (<code>teal-800</code>
          in light, <code>teal-200</code> in dark), because hover on a dark
          surface gets lighter, not darker.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)"
            >Check every pair that is read together, in both modes.</strong
          >
          A token never has a contrast ratio on its own; a text token on a
          background token does. List the pairs (body text on each surface, a
          label on its fill, status text on its tinted background) and measure
          each one in light and dark.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        Atelier does that with <code>npm run check:contrast</code>
        (<code>node tools/scripts/wcag-contrast.mjs --check</code>). The script
        reads <code>libs/styles/src/tokens.css</code>, resolves
        <code>var()</code> aliases inside each mode, and measures a hand-written
        list of <code>[foreground, background, role, note]</code> pairs in
        light, dark, and both <code>data-theme</code> variants. Role is
        <code>normal</code> (4.5:1), <code>large</code> or <code>ui</code>
        (3:1), or <code>decorative</code> (reported, not failed). A pair that
        names a token the source does not define is a gate failure, not a
        skipped row.
      </p>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        In your own repo you do not need Atelier's tooling for this. The script
        has no dependencies: copy it, point the token-file path at your CSS, and
        replace the pair list with yours. If your tokens live in Figma or a JSON
        file instead, the same idea holds: one list of pairs, a ratio per mode,
        a non-zero exit when one fails.
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">Worked example: a knockout state</h3>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        An alert needs an inverted (knockout) variant: a saturated fill with
        light text, for error, warning, success and info. The component already
        has a fill token for each status, but a label placed on the fill had no
        token to read.
      </p>
      <ol
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.7;
          margin: 0 0 1rem;
          padding-left: 1.25rem;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)"
            >Find the gap by name.</strong
          >
          The role list has <code>danger-bg</code> and <code>danger-text</code>
          for the tinted style, but nothing for text or border on a solid fill.
          The only way to render the knockout variant is a raw value, and that
          is exactly what the agent will write.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)"
            >Add the tokens in the UI tier, by role.</strong
          >
          Following the pattern above, one content and one border token per
          status, named for the role they play on a knockout fill (the course
          session called them <em>error knockout</em>, <em>warning knockout</em
          >, <em>success knockout</em> and <em>info knockout</em>). They alias
          lighter ramp steps so they sit on the dark fill.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)"
            >Keep border and content distinct.</strong
          >
          Border gets a darker step than content, so the edge does not merge
          with the label.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)"
            >Define both modes.</strong
          >
          Write the dark values in the same commit; a token with only a light
          value is a bug that shows up at night.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)"
            >Add the pairs, then measure.</strong
          >
          Add each content token on its fill to the contrast list as a
          <code>normal</code> pair, and the border on its fill as a
          <code>ui</code> pair. Run the check; adjust the ramp step until every
          pair passes in both modes.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong style="color: var(--ui-color-text)"
            >Only then touch the component.</strong
          >
          The component binds the new tokens. The order is the lesson: token
