    <!-- Token pipeline -->
    <div class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="pipeline" class="docs-section-title">
        The token pipeline in practice
      </h2>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        For your own design system you have to settle three things before you
        write a single variable: where tokens are <strong>authored</strong>, how
        they <strong>travel</strong> between Figma and code, and which side wins
        when the two disagree. Teams that skip this end up with two files that
        both claim to be the source of truth.
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3">The vocabulary</h3>
      <ul
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.7;
          margin: 0 0 1rem;
          padding-left: 1.25rem;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        <li>
          <strong>DTCG</strong> is the W3C Design Tokens format: tokens as JSON.
          In the course it is described as platform-agnostic, so with the right
          transforms the same file can become web CSS custom properties, iOS or
          Android values.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Style Dictionary</strong> is the tool the course names for
          those transforms: JSON tokens in, one output per platform out.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>figma-console-mcp</strong> ships its own pair of tools for
          this. <code>figma_export_tokens</code> writes Figma variables out as
          DTCG JSON or other formats (CSS variables, Tailwind v3 and v4, SCSS, a
          TypeScript module, Style Dictionary v3 and Tokens Studio layouts).
          <code>figma_import_tokens</code> pushes tokens from code into Figma as
          variables.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">The course's round trip</h3>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        In the DSAI jam session the export is described as deterministic: the
        variables table becomes DTCG JSON, and a second step renders it in the
        style you ask for, Tailwind v4 for example. The speaker's summary is
        that this is "basically Style Dictionary built into" figma-console-mcp.
        Import is the reverse, for a team that starts from a code-first
        canonical source. Because both directions are deterministic, a changed
        value is matched to that exact variable and updated, rather than
        everything being rewritten. A third course session shows the other entry
        point: asking the agent to create new variables straight in Figma
        through the same server.
      </p>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        That session also shows what a token gap looks like. An inverted
        (<strong>knockout</strong>) background for error, warning, success and
        info states had backgrounds but no matching content and border colours,
        so a label became illegible on it. The fix was new
        <em>error knockout</em>, <em>warning knockout</em>,
        <em>success knockout</em> and <em>info knockout</em> content and border
        tokens in the semantic tier, mapped to lighter colours so they sit on
        the dark background. Border got a darker step than content so the two
        stay distinct. The lesson is not the colour names but the order: add the
        token first, and the component follows.
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">How Atelier does it: code first</h3>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        Atelier authors tokens by hand in one CSS file and projects them
        outward. Nothing in the repo pulls a Figma edit back into code. The
        scripts and their headers say so, and ADR-0030 records the reason: the
        repo's gates already treat code as canonical and project outward, so
        tokens follow the same direction, and a hand-edited Figma collection is
        drift by definition.
      </p>
      <table class="docs-props-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Step</th>
            <th>Command</th>
            <th>What it does</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Author</td>
            <td><code>libs/styles/src/tokens.css</code></td>
            <td>
              The one hand-edited file. Light values, dark values and the
              <code>--ui-*</code> names live here.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Copy to code</td>
            <td><code>npm run sync:tokens</code></td>
            <td>
              Copies it byte-for-byte into each framework library, the
              create-workspace preset and the design skill's sheet.
              <code>npm run check:tokens</code> fails on drift.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Push to Figma</td>
            <td><code>npm run figma:sync-tokens</code></td>
            <td>
              <code>gen-figma-library-tokens.mjs</code> parses the CSS into
              variable definitions (<code>--ui-color-text</code> becomes
              <code>color/text</code>, rem becomes px, <code>var()</code>
              becomes an alias). <code>figma-sync-library-tokens.mjs</code>
              upserts them into the <code>Library Tokens</code> collection
              through figma-console-mcp's <code>figma_execute</code>. It reports
              variables that exist only in Figma but does not delete them.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Record</td>
            <td><code>npm run figma:snapshot</code></td>
            <td>
              Refreshes the committed Figma snapshot the offline gates read. The
              sync script prints this as the next step, followed by
              <code>npm run check:figma</code>.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin: 1rem 0;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        Two things are deliberately left out of the Figma projection: shadows,
        easings, durations, letter-spacing, z-index and the composite focus ring
        are not simple variables, so the generator skips them. And the push
        needs Figma Desktop with the file open and the figma-console Desktop
        Bridge plugin running, so it is run by hand and is not part of
        <code>check:all</code>.
      </p>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">The gates behind it</h3>
      <table class="docs-props-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Gate</th>
            <th>What it keeps honest</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><code>npm run check:tokens</code></td>
            <td>Every copy of <code>tokens.css</code> equals the source.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>npm run check:figma-token-names</code></td>
            <td>
              Every <code>color/*</code>, <code>spacing/*</code> and
              <code>radius/*</code> variable in the Figma snapshot maps back to
              a declared <code>--ui-*</code> token. It checks names, not values,
              and only in that direction, because code tokens legitimately exist
              before Figma catches up.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>npm run check:token-annotations</code></td>
            <td>
              Every declared token has an entry in
              <code>libs/spec/src/tokens.manifest.ts</code> with an intent and
              constraints, the agent-readable layer that says what a token is
              for, not just its value.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">Which approach fits your system</h3>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
          max-width: 60ch;
        "
      >
        The export/import route is the better start when you do not want to own
        a pipeline. You get DTCG and several output formats from a maintained
        tool, you can start in either place, and the diff-based merge means a
        single edited value produces a single update. Atelier wrote its own
        scripts for a narrower reason: its source is already plain CSS custom
        properties, and the import tool's documentation lists CSS custom
        properties as accepted but not yet implemented, with DTCG as the fully
        supported input. Going through it would mean first converting the CSS to
        DTCG. The price of the hand-rolled path is that you maintain the name
        mapping, the skip list and the drift gates yourself. Pick one direction
        as the source of truth and write it down; the rest follows from that
        choice.
      </p>
      <Callout tone="note" title="Use the same server for both.">
        The sync script talks to the same figma-console-mcp server you use in an
        agent session. The difference is who is driving: a script with a fixed
        payload is repeatable and reviewable, while a prompt like the knockout
        example is the quick way to explore a new token before you commit it to
        the CSS.
      </Callout>
    </div>

    <!-- Pink test -->
    <div class="docs-section">
      <h2 id="pink-test" class="docs-section-title">Exercise: the pink test</h2>
      <p
