      <h3 class="docs-h3">In Figma</h3>
      <ol
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.7;
          margin: 0 0 1rem;
          padding-left: 1.25rem;
        "
      >
        <li>
          Open the <code>Library Tokens</code> collection (the generated
          semantic tier with <strong>Light</strong> and <strong>Dark</strong>
          modes) in file <code>QMnDD8uZQPldPrlCwZZ58T</code> and add a third
          mode, <code>Pink Test</code>. Through figma-console-mcp that is
          <code>figma_add_mode</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Set the colour variables in the new mode to one loud pink, for example
          <code>#ff00c8</code>. Batch the edit rather than clicking through the
          list.
        </li>
        <li>
          Put a test frame holding instances of the masters on the
          <code>Pink Test</code> mode (Layer → Appearance → Variable modes).
          Component Tokens are single-mode aliases into Library Tokens, so they
          follow the frame's mode.
        </li>
        <li>
          Read the frame: every fill, stroke and text colour that is still its
          original colour is a raw value or a binding to the wrong tier. Fix the
          binding on the master, not the instance.
        </li>
        <li>
          Delete the <code>Pink Test</code> mode. It is an instrument, not a
          theme, and <code>Library Tokens</code> is generated from
          <code>tokens.css</code>, so a mode nobody generated is drift by
          definition (ADR-0030).
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">In code</h3>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-bottom: 1rem;
        "
      >
        The code twin costs nothing: the UI tier is plain CSS custom properties,
        so override every <code>--ui-color-*</code> on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code>
        in the document that renders the component (this docs app, or a story's
        preview) and look for anything that does not change. Run this in the
        browser console. An inline style on <code>&lt;html&gt;</code> wins over
        both the default and the <code>data-theme</code> blocks in
        <code>tokens.css</code>:
      </p>
      <div class="docs-code-block">
        <div class="docs-code-block-header">
          <span class="docs-code-block-lang">js</span>
        </div>
        <pre><code>{"const pink = '#ff00c8';\nfor (const sheet of document.styleSheets) {\n  let rules;\n  try { rules = sheet.cssRules; } catch { continue; } // cross-origin sheet\n  for (const rule of rules) {\n    for (const name of rule.style ?? []) {\n      if (name.startsWith('--ui-color-')) {\n        document.documentElement.style.setProperty(name, pink);\n      }\n    }\n  }\n}"}</code></pre>
        <p style="color: var(--ui-color-text-muted); line-height: 1.6">
          Give it a second: components that animate their colours (the button
          does) fade to pink rather than switching at once.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin: 1rem 0;
        "
      >
        Text, surfaces and borders now share one colour, so the page looks
        broken. That is the point: whatever kept its teal, grey or white takes
        its colour from somewhere other than a <code>--ui-color-*</code> token.
        Reload to undo it.
      </p>

      <Callout
        tone="note"
        title="The Storybook theme control is not this test."
      >
        The toolbar theme control added in ADR-0142 only flips
        <code>data-theme</code> between <code>light</code> and <code>dark</code
        >. It checks that both token blocks resolve, not that a component reads
        tokens at all, so use the console snippet for the pink test.
      </Callout>

      <h3 class="docs-h3 docs-h3--spaced">The gates that catch it for you</h3>
      <table class="docs-props-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Pink test finding</th>
            <th>Automatic twin</th>
            <th>Scope</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>A colour that stays put in code</td>
            <td>
              <code>npm run check:stylelint</code>, rule
              <code>atelier/no-raw-color-literal</code>
            </td>
            <td>
              Hex, <code>rgb()</code> and <code>hsl()</code> literals in
              component and docs stylesheets. A <code>var(--token, #fff)</code>
              fallback is allowed.
            </td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>A fill or stroke that stays put in Figma</td>
            <td><code>npm run check:figma</code>, token-link coverage</td>
            <td>
              Raw hex fills and strokes on masters, read offline from the
              committed snapshot <code>tools/figma/snapshot.json</code>, not
              from the live file.
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p
        style="
          color: var(--ui-color-text-muted);
          line-height: 1.6;
          margin-top: 1rem;
        "
      >
        Run the pink test once after a re-architecture or a bulk token change,
        when you want to see the result. The gates run on every
        <code>check:all</code>. A mode added to <code>Library Tokens</code> also
        counts against your Figma plan's mode limit, because every mode
        duplicates every value in its collection and Figma caps modes per plan
        tier (ADR-0018).
      </p>
    </div>

